@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# OpenCode Go
+
+[OpenCode Go](https://opencode.ai/docs/go) is OpenCode's flat-rate subscription to curated open
+coding models: one API key, bounded monthly cost, and a catalog that changes as models are tested
+and added. The same subscription serves its models over three protocol endpoints, so meka models it
+as three backends, one per protocol:
+
+| Backend | Protocol | Current model families (see below) |
+|---------|----------|-------------------------------------|
+| `opencode-go` | OpenAI Chat Completions | GLM, Kimi, DeepSeek, MiMo, LongCat, Hy, Omen |
+| `opencode-go-responses` | OpenAI Responses | Grok, GPT-5.6 Luna, Muse Spark |
+| `opencode-go-messages` | Anthropic Messages | MiniMax, Qwen |
+
+The per-model endpoint is OpenCode's own routing: each model is served on one of the three, and a
+request in the wrong shape is rejected. The mapping changes as the catalog does, so meka does not
+bundle a copy; OpenCode's [Go docs](https://opencode.ai/docs/go) list the current table. `meka
+account add` offers all three, and `meka profile add` suggests the current headline model of each
+family (`kimi-k3`, `gpt-5.6-luna`, `minimax-m3`).
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Setting | Value |
+|---------|-------|
+| Account `backend` | `opencode-go`, `opencode-go-responses`, or `opencode-go-messages` |
+| Default base URL | `https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1` |
+| Credential | API key from the [OpenCode console](https://opencode.ai/auth), kept in the store |
+| Auth method | Bearer token (`Authorization: Bearer <key>`) |
+
+The same API key works for every backend, so one subscription can reach all three protocol families
+through three accounts:
+
+```bash
+meka account add oc-go --backend opencode-go
+meka account add oc-go-responses --backend opencode-go-responses
+meka account add oc-go-messages --backend opencode-go-messages
+meka profile add coding --account oc-go --model kimi-k3
+```
+
+### Config file
+
+The commands write this for you (the key stays in the store, not here):
+
+```toml
+[accounts.oc-go]
+backend = "opencode-go"
+
+[accounts.oc-go-responses]
+backend = "opencode-go-responses"
+
+[accounts.oc-go-messages]
+backend = "opencode-go-messages"
+
+[profiles.coding]
+account = "oc-go"
+model   = "kimi-k3"
+```
+
+## The session header
+
+OpenCode's docs require every inference request to carry a stable per-conversation id in
+`x-opencode-session`, which the gateway uses to route a conversation's requests to one upstream and
+to prompt-cache its prefix. Requests without it fail. meka sends the session's own id, the same
+uuid a resume keeps, on both streaming and non-streaming requests, alongside a `meka/<version>`
+user agent. No configuration is needed; the header is part of what these three backends are.
+
+## Usage
+
+`meka account usage` shows the three dollar-budget windows every Go subscription has, read from the
+gateway's usage endpoint with the account's API key:
+
+```console
+$ meka account usage oc-go
+Usage for 'oc-go':
+  5-hour (rolling)  41.5%
+  Weekly            12.0%
+  Monthly           58.0%
+```
+
+The windows reset on the gateway's schedule; a window at 100% is a `rate-limited` subscription
+until it does. When the account also has Zen balance with **Use balance** enabled, requests fall
+back to it instead of failing.
+
+## Supported models
+
+Any model OpenCode Go serves on the protocol the backend speaks; the model string is forwarded
+verbatim. Get the current catalog and its per-model endpoint from OpenCode's Go docs above, and
+see also `GET https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models` for the machine-readable id list. A profile's
+`context_window` defaults to 1M, which covers every current Go model; state a smaller window on the
+profile if you want compaction to fire earlier.
\ No newline at end of file
